/// Direction from the body TOWARD the sun, in world space. +Z is behind the viewer (the
/// bodies sit at -Z), +X is the viewer's right.
///
/// BEHIND THE VIEWER, slightly to their right — Rick's call from the headset, and it is
/// also what makes Saturn's rings appear at all. The old value was (-1.0, 0.45, 0.55):
/// up and to the left. Saturn's ring plane carries the globe's 26.7° tilt, so its normal
/// is (0.449, 0, 0.893), and against that old direction N·L came to **+0.034** — the rings
/// were lit at 88° grazing incidence, i.e. ~3% brightness, which against a black sky is
/// indistinguishable from not being drawn. That is the "rings didn't render at all" report;
/// the geometry was always fine (doubleSided, opacity wired to the texture's alpha).
/// Here N·L = +0.99, so the ring face the viewer is on is the face that gets lit.
///
/// Y is a token +0.10 purely so the terminator isn't a perfectly vertical line; set it to
/// 0 for a dead-flat "full phase" look. The cost of lighting from behind the viewer is that
/// every body reads as FULL phase — no dramatic terminator, and for Saturn the ring shadow
/// falls behind the rings from this angle, so the §7c bake's whole feature stops being
/// visible. That is a deliberate trade, not an oversight.
private static let sunDirection = SIMD3<Float>(0.40, 0.10, 1.0)

/// Directional key light for the unbaked bodies. There is no IBL in a full immersive space,
/// so without this a UsdPreviewSurface body renders black.
private static func makeSun(target: SIMD3<Float>) -> Entity {
    let sun = DirectionalLight()
    sun.name = sunName
    // Bright: these are albedo maps of sunlit bodies, and the surround is pure black, so an
    // interior-lighting default reads as dusk. Peak illumination is unchanged by the
    // direction move (the sub-solar point was already on the disc), so this does not clip
    // anything it didn't clip before — but the MEAN is higher now that most of the visible
    // disc faces the sun, so if the bodies read hot in the headset, this is the dial.
    sun.light.intensity = 3000
    sun.light.color = .white
    sun.shadow = nil    // one convex body, nothing to receive a shadow it doesn't already own
    // `look` aims the entity's -Z (a DirectionalLight's emission direction) at `target`.
    sun.look(at: target,
             from: target + normalize(sunDirection) * distance,
             relativeTo: nil)
    return sun
}

    // The sun goes in for EVERY body, including the baked one — see `unlit`. Saturn's
    // rings are a separate material from the globe and were never baked, so on the baked
    // body they were receiving no light whatsoever and rendered pure black. That is the
    // second, independent half of the "rings didn't render" report and the reason the
    // sun-direction fix alone would not have fixed the baked card.
    root.addChild(makeSun(target: holder.position))
    return true
}

