/// BEHIND THE VIEWER, a little right and above — Rick's direction from the headset, and
/// simultaneously the angle that lights Saturn's rings as brightly as physics allows.
///
/// The ring annulus inherits the globe's 26.7° tilt AND the USDZ root Xform's −90°
/// Z-up→Y-up conversion, which together put its normal at **(0.449, 0.893, 0.000)** in
/// RealityKit axes. Two things follow, and both matter:
///
/// 1. The old value (−1.0, 0.45, 0.55) gave N·L = **−0.039** — the sun sat 2.2° off the
///    ring PLANE, lighting the annulus edge-on from very slightly behind. The rings were
///    drawn and were effectively black, which is the "rings didn't render at all" report.
///    The geometry was never at fault: doubleSided = 1, opacity wired to the texture alpha.
///
/// 2. N_z is exactly 0, so ring brightness does NOT depend on how far behind the viewer
///    the sun is — only on its left/right and up/down. Putting the sun behind the viewer
///    is therefore free, and the whole budget can go to elevation above the ring plane.
///
/// Saturn's rings lie in its equatorial plane, so the sun can never be more than 26.7°
/// above them — |N·L| ≤ sin(26.7°) = 0.449 is a hard physical ceiling, not a tuning
/// choice. This vector is the minimum-norm solution that reaches that ceiling exactly
/// while keeping the largest possible +Z: 26.7° off the view axis, 12.7° right, 23.7° up,
/// N·L = 0.449. Anything brighter would be a sun in an orbit Saturn does not have. An
/// earlier attempt at (0.40, 0.10, 1.0) reached only 0.249 — visible, but 55% of this.
///
/// Cost, deliberately accepted: at 26.7° off-axis the bodies read close to full phase, and
/// Saturn's ring shadow falls behind the rings, so the §7c bake's feature does not show.
private static let sunDirection = SIMD3<Float>(0.2019, 0.4014, 0.8934)
